{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on 9f9ffef8, successor of 74d1f7b3 (planner, 2026-10-21): slice 9 production authority, token and repository.\n\n1. Read authority. The archiver is host code, as the destination's Git adapter is. It is not a member and holds no session, and it never uses the inspector or the public readers port. Its authority is the room's admitted archive binding (S2a, fb390d88): the binding in the room's directory names the archived scope, and the directory's recorded repository names the target. With that binding, the host reads exactly two things through the namespace's in-process item reads: the directory's repository item and the destination's branch item for the archive ref. Each read is charged to the pass's archive budget. Anything else is refused: another item type, another scope, a scope whose directory is not under the host's pinned register (host-wiring hostBound), and a directory with no admitted binding. Witnesses: a pass reads the two items; a binding for a directory outside the pinned register, and a query for any other item, are refused. Extend archive-installed.test.ts for these production seams, as the request proposes; production permission is not inferred from the fixture.\n\n2. Token. The Artifacts binding is the mint authority. For each pass, the archiver mints one write token for the room's own repository with get(name).createToken(\"write\", 900), keeps the service-reported expiry, uses the token only for that pass's push, and revokes it when the pass ends, or lets it expire when the service has no revoke. The final send guard on scope, ref and repository stays. No static Worker secret is used for archive writes. The \"secret (planner sets)\" in the slice 9 request came from the earlier plan for a separate private repository; decision 7d947a6b replaced that plan, so no archive secret exists, and the planner sets none. This meets the note's short-lived per-pass provider token (section 15.5) without a fabricated destination binding or ledger custody.\n\n3. Repository. The archive goes to the room's own recorded repository (decision 7d947a6b), under refs/artroom/journal/\u003cscope ID\u003e. For the hosted grow demo that is host Artifacts (the deployment's ARTIFACTS_CONFIG host), namespace artroom-demo, name ryghgistvpshbaznxlbleop5zqruy2kmogy4jeigr7j3yqlewceq-1, the repository of directory sc_ryghgistvpshbaznxlbleop5zqruy2kmogy4jeigr7j3yqlewceq. Read its repository ID from the directory's repository item; do not copy it from this decision. There is no public locator. The archive is readable by whoever can clone the room's repository: a member, through dap clone and its short-lived read token. Offline replay starts from that clone. The /grow/ page says that the room's journal is archived in its repository and that members can clone it; it gives no public clone URL. Register17 and Membership8 journals stay excluded; the caller anchors and omitted-text qualifications stay explicit.\n\nBuilder stays local. Provisioning checks, binding, deployment and readbacks remain the planner's (2fcf3c3a), after review of the exact head.","body":{"to":"builder"}}